NAME
       likeos-gpt - show, check and edit GUID partition tables

SYNOPSIS
       likeos-gpt [options] print device
       likeos-gpt [options] verify device
       likeos-gpt [options] info device n
       likeos-gpt [options] new-table device
       likeos-gpt  [options]  add  device  [-n  n] [--start pos] [--size size]
       [--type type] [--name name] [--uuid guid] [--attrs hex]
       likeos-gpt [options] delete device n
       likeos-gpt [options] resize device n size|max
       likeos-gpt [options] name|type|attrs device n value
       likeos-gpt [options] repair|wipe device
       likeos-gpt [options] types

DESCRIPTION
       likeos-gpt reads and writes the GUID partition table (GPT) of  a  disk:
       the  protective  MBR in the first sector, the primary table (header and
       partition entries) after it, and the backup copy  at  the  end  of  the
       disk.   It  is  the tool of the LikeOS installer's partitioning library
       (libgpt) for the command line: everything the installer does to a  par-
       tition table can be looked at and done by hand with it.

       device is a block device (/dev/sda, /dev/nvme0n1) or a disk image file.
       Only  the  editing commands write, and only to the device named.  After
       writing to a block device likeos-gpt asks the kernel to  read  the  new
       table  again,  so  the partition devices (/dev/sda1, /dev/nvme0n1p1...)
       follow at once; a disk with a mounted partition may  refuse  that,  and
       the change is then seen after the next start.

COMMANDS
       print device
              Show  the  table: the disk's size, sector size and GUID, the us-
              able sectors, and one line per partition with its number,  first
              and last sector, size, type and name.

       verify device
              Check  both copies of the table: the header and entry checksums,
              the positions the headers give, overlapping or out-of-range par-
              titions, and whether the two copies agree.  Problems found  give
              exit status 1.

       info device n
              Show  partition n in detail: its type (name, code and GUID), its
              unique partition GUID, the first and last sector, the size,  the
              name and the attribute bits.

       new-table device
              Write a new, empty GPT with a protective MBR.  A device that al-
              ready has partitions is refused unless --yes is given.

       add device
              Create  a partition, by default in the first free region, start-
              ing at the first 1 MiB boundary in it and filling  the  rest  of
              it.  The add options below choose otherwise.

       delete device n
              Delete partition n.  The data on the disk is not touched.

       resize device n size|max
              Move  the end of partition n: size is its new size, max makes it
              reach the next partition or the end of the usable sectors.   The
              start stays where it is, and the file system in the partition is
              not  changed:  grow  the file system after growing the partition
              (for ext4 resize2fs(8)), shrink it before shrinking  the  parti-
              tion.

       name device n name
              Set  the  name  of  partition  n  (UTF-8, at most 36 UTF-16 code
              units).

       type device n type
              Set the type of partition n.

       attrs device n hex
              Set the 64 attribute bits of partition n, as a hexadecimal  num-
              ber  (bit  0:  required  by  the platform, bit 1: ignored by the
              firmware, bit 2: legacy BIOS bootable, bits  48-63:  defined  by
              the partition type).

       repair device
              Rebuild  a damaged copy of the table from the intact one, move a
              backup copy that is not at the end of the  disk  (a  disk  image
              that was made larger, a disk copied to a bigger one) to the end,
              and rewrite a missing or wrong protective MBR.  When both copies
              are intact but differ, the primary one is kept.

       wipe device
              Erase  the  partition table: both GPT copies and the MBR.  Needs
              --yes.

       types  List the partition types likeos-gpt knows by name: alias,  four-
              digit code, GUID and description.

ADD OPTIONS
       -n, --number n
              The partition number (default: the first unused one).

       -s, --start pos
              The  first  sector (default: the start of the first free region,
              aligned to 1 MiB).

       -S, --size size
              The size (default: the rest of the free region it starts in).

       -t, --type type
              The partition type (default: data).

       -c, --name name
              The partition name.

       -u, --uuid guid
              The unique partition GUID (default: a random one).

       -A, --attrs hex
              The attribute bits.

OPTIONS
       --json Machine-readable output (JSON) of print, verify, info and types,
              and of the partition add created.

       --sector-size 512|4096
              The logical sector size of a disk image file (block devices  re-
              port their own).

       -y, --yes
              Confirm  a  command  that  destroys a partition table: new-table
              over an existing table, and wipe.

       --no-reread
              Do not ask the kernel to read the table again after writing it.

       -h, --help
              Show a summary of the commands and options.

       --version
              Show the version.

SIZES, POSITIONS AND TYPES
       A position or size that is a plain number counts sectors.  With a  suf-
       fix  it  counts bytes: K, M, G, T (or KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB) are powers of
       1024, so 512M is 512 MiB.

       A type is an alias, a four-digit code or a GUID.  The common ones:

              esp           ef00  EFI system partition
              likeos-root   8304  LikeOS root file system
              data          8300  generic data
              swap          8200  swap
              bios-boot     ef02  BIOS boot
              home          8302  home directories
              basic         0700  basic data (NTFS, exFAT, FAT)
              msr           0c01  reserved (MSR)
              recovery      2700  recovery environment
              lvm, raid     8e00, fd00

       likeos-gpt types lists them all.

EXIT STATUS
       0      Success.

       1      The command failed, or verify found problems.

       2      Wrong usage.

EXAMPLES
       Partition a disk the way the installer does for a whole-disk  installa-
       tion:

              likeos-gpt --yes new-table /dev/sdb
              likeos-gpt add /dev/sdb --size 512M --type esp \
                      --name "EFI system partition"
              likeos-gpt add /dev/sdb --type likeos-root --name likeos-root

       Check a disk image and repair it after it was made larger:

              likeos-gpt verify disk.img
              likeos-gpt repair disk.img

SEE ALSO
       likeos-install(1), fdisk(8), sfdisk(8), partx(8), blkid(8), lsblk(8)

